# =====================================================================================
# quasigroup_law.rq — RED->GREEN probe for T-QGRP (the LATIN-DIVISION / unique-division tooth,
# W2 · SP0, Stage 2a). The added-law tooth of the quasigroup rung: the sole law quasigroup
# adds over magma is LATIN-DIVISION — for all a,b in S there exist UNIQUE x,y in S with
# a·x = b and y·a = b, equivalently the Cayley table is a LATIN SQUARE (algebra_spine.ttl
# fnd:lawLatinDivision; design §1.5, plan T-QGRP).
# =====================================================================================
# UNIQUE DIVISION as a graph fact over the reified Cayley table. The Latin-square property
# fails exactly when division is NOT unique — i.e. two DISTINCT cells of the SAME model
# collide on a division:
#   (i) LEFT-division collision  : same first operand a and same result b, different second
#       operands (a·x1 = a·x2 = b with x1 != x2) -> a\b is not unique -> the element b occurs
#       TWICE in row a of the table (a Latin-square row violation);
#   (ii) RIGHT-division collision : same second operand a and same result b, different first
#       operands (x1·a = x2·a = b with x1 != x2) -> b/a is not unique -> b occurs TWICE in
#       column a (a Latin-square column violation).
# This SELECT returns every such collision — the model and the two offending cells.
#
#   GREEN (a genuine quasigroup, e.g. fixtures/quasigroup_pos.ttl — Z/3 under +, a Latin square
#         over carrier {e,a,b}): 0 rows — every row and every column is a permutation of the
#         carrier, so division is total and unique.
#   RED   (a broken quasigroup, e.g. fixtures/quasigroup_neg.ttl — the cell e·a is changed from
#         a to b, so row e reads e·e=e, e·a=b, e·b=b: b occurs twice, a is missing):
#         >= 1 row — e·a and e·b share first operand e and result b with distinct second
#         operands, a left-division collision, so the Latin-division tooth bites.
#
# Run over the fixture under test (optionally merged with the algebra data graph), inference=none.
# The SAME single injection (e·a -> b) that this probe catches ALSO flips the committed
# shapes/algebra.shapes.ttl ModelShape True->False: the collision is placed on the e-row and the
# witness declares @rel:identity:element e (a quasigroup may HAVE an identity — that makes it a
# loop, a legitimate quasigroup witness, just as fixtures/magma_pos.ttl is a magma that is also a
# monoid), so the left-unit tooth e·x = x reads e·a = b != a and ModelShape reddens too — so the
# runner's fixture-polarity gate catches the negative even though the dedicated QuasigroupShape is
# a Stage-2 T-SHAPES deliverable and shapes/algebra.shapes.ttl is out of this rung's write scope
# (design §1.5, §2c; the latin-division axiom is spine/quasigroup.ttl kingdom:axiom:…:class:
# division:…:family:quasigroup:genus:latin:species:unique:…).

PREFIX rel: <urn:silmaril:type:graph:instance:instruction:code:property:relation:>

# One top-level FILTER (NOT a bare-FILTER-under-UNION group): a bare FILTER inside a
# { } UNION branch scopes only to that branch's own bindings, so operand variables bound in
# the enclosing group are not visible there and the tooth would never bite. The two collision
# cases are therefore expressed as a single disjunction over ?r1 = ?r2 (same result):
#   (i)  LEFT-division  collision : ?f1 = ?f2 && ?s1 != ?s2  (same first operand, different second)
#   (ii) RIGHT-division collision : ?s1 = ?s2 && ?f1 != ?f2  (same second operand, different first)
SELECT ?model ?cell1 ?cell2 WHERE {
  ?cell1 rel:structure ?model ;
         rel:category:first  ?f1 ;
         rel:category:second ?s1 ;
         rel:category:result ?r1 .
  ?cell2 rel:structure ?model ;
         rel:category:first  ?f2 ;
         rel:category:second ?s2 ;
         rel:category:result ?r2 .
  FILTER( ?cell1 != ?cell2 && ?r1 = ?r2 &&
          ( ( ?f1 = ?f2 && ?s1 != ?s2 ) || ( ?s1 = ?s2 && ?f1 != ?f2 ) ) )
}
